You are not required to comply with both. Choose whichever you prefer.

The corresponding SPDX expression, as declared in package.json, in REUSE.toml
and in the per-file headers, is:

    MIT OR Apache-2.0

Why two licenses
----------------

Apache-2.0 grants everything MIT does and adds an explicit patent grant
(section 3), which protects downstream users from patent litigation by
contributors. MIT is shorter and more permissive about attribution. Offering
both is the convention across the Rust ecosystem, which is the ecosystem this
action serves, so a project already dual-licensed that way takes on nothing new
by using it.

Note this repository publishes a GitHub Action, not a crate: there is no
package on crates.io and none on npm. What you consume is a git tag. README.md
lists what each tag points at, and SECURITY.md which of them receive fixes.

Why two copies of each license
------------------------------

LICENSES/Apache-2.0.txt and LICENSES/MIT.txt are where the REUSE Specification
requires license texts to live, and they are what `comply lint` reads.
LICENSE-APACHE and LICENSE-MIT at the repository root are byte-identical copies,
kept because that is where GitHub and most tooling look. Copies rather than
symlinks: a symlink survives git, but not every consumer of an exported archive
or a Windows checkout resolves one.

Contribution
------------

Unless you state otherwise, any contribution you intentionally submit for
inclusion in this work, as defined in the Apache-2.0 license, shall be
dual-licensed as above, with no additional terms or conditions.

No Developer Certificate of Origin sign-off is required: no DCO bot runs on
pull requests and no `Signed-off-by` trailer is checked. CONTRIBUTING.md has
what is actually expected of a contribution.

Per-file licensing
------------------

Every file in this repository declares its own licensing, either in an SPDX
header or through an annotation in REUSE.toml, following the REUSE
Specification. Run `comply lint` to verify, or consult REUSE.toml for the
aggregate rules — its `path = ["**"]` annotation is what licenses the whole
tree, documentation included, under the same MIT OR Apache-2.0 expression.
